[titledesc {Encryption via one-time pad}]
[require Tcl "8.6 9"]
[require tcl::transform::core [opt 1.1]]
[require tcl::transform::otp [opt 1.1]]
[description]
[para]

The [package tcl::transform::otp] package provides a command creating
a channel transformation which uses externally provided one-time pads
to perform encryption (on writing) and decryption (on reading).

[para] A related transformation in this module is
[package tcl::transform::rot].

[para] The internal [package TclOO] class implementing the transform
handler is a sub-class of the [package tcl::transform::core]
framework.

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::tcl::transform::otp] [arg chan] [arg keychanw] [arg keychanr]]

This command creates a one-time pad based encryption transformation on
top of the channel [arg chan] and returns [arg chan].

[para] The two channels [arg keychanw] and [arg keychanr] contain the
one-time pads for the write and read directions, respectively. For
every byte written to or read from [arg chan] the transformation reads
one byte from the respective pad channel and xors the two. As xor is
its own inverse, the same operation both encrypts and decrypts: data
written with a pad is recovered by reading it back with a channel
positioned at the same place in the same pad.

[para] The pad channels have to be readable and configured for binary
data (e.g. opened with mode [const rb]), and have to provide at least
as many bytes as pass through the transformation. When a pad runs out,
the transformation fails with an error. The pad channels are neither
seeked nor closed by the transformation; this is left to the caller.

[para] The transformation is meant for streaming. Seeking [arg chan]
while the transformation is active does not reposition the pads.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

Encrypt the file [file plain.txt] into [file secret.bin], then decrypt
it again by reading [file secret.bin] through the transformation. The
pad file [file pad.bin] has to contain at least as many random bytes as
[file plain.txt], e.g. taken from [file /dev/urandom]. Each direction
gets its own channel to the pad, so both start at the beginning of the
pad.

[example {
package require tcl::transform::otp

# Encrypt.
set in  [open plain.txt rb]
set out [open secret.bin wb]
set kw  [open pad.bin rb]
set kr  [open pad.bin rb]
tcl::transform::otp $out $kw $kr
fcopy $in $out
close $out
close $in
close $kw
close $kr

# Decrypt.
set in [open secret.bin rb]
set kw [open pad.bin rb]
set kr [open pad.bin rb]
tcl::transform::otp $in $kw $kr
set data [read $in]
close $in
close $kw
close $kr
}]

